Let $x(t) = u(t) e^{-t}$. Then
\begin{align*} \dot{x} &= u'(t) e^{-t} - u(t) e^{-2t} \\ \ddot{x} &= u''(t) e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} \end{align*}
Substituting into the differential equation $\ddot{x}+3 \dot{x}+2 x=t e^{-t}$, we have
\begin{align*} u''(t) e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} + 3(u'(t) e^{-t} - u(t) e^{-2t}) + 2u(t) e^{-t} &= t e^{-t} \\ u''(t) e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} + 3u'(t) e^{-t} - 3u(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-t} &= t e^{-t} \\ u''(t) e^{-t} + u'(t) e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} - 3u(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} + 2u(t) e^{-t} &= t e^{-t} \\ u''(t) e^{-t} + u'(t) e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} - 3u(t) e^{-2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} + 2u(t) e^{-t} &= t e^{-t} \end{align*}
We want to eliminate the terms with $e^{-2t}$ and $e^{-3t}$. We can multiply by $e^{2t}$ to get
\begin{align*} u''(t) e^{-t} e^{2t} + u'(t) e^{-t} e^{2t} - 2u'(t) e^{-2t} e^{2t} - 3u(t) e^{-2t} e^{2t} + 2u(t) e^{-3t} e^{2t} + 2u(t) e^{-t} e^{2t} &= t e^{-t} e^{2t} \\ u''(t) e^{t} + u'(t) e^{t} - 2u'(t) + 3u(t) - 2u(t) e^{t} + 2u(t) e^{t} &= t e^{t} \\ u''(t) e^{t} + u'(t) e^{t} - 2u'(t) + 3u(t) &= t e^{t} \end{align*}
We want to eliminate the terms with $e^{t}$. We can multiply by $e^{-t}$ to get
\begin{align*} u''(t) e^{t} e^{-t} + u'(t) e^{t} e^{-t} - 2u'(t) e^{t} e^{-t} + 3u(t) e^{t} e^{-t} &= t e^{t} e^{-t} \\ u''(t) + u'(t) - 2u'(t) + 3u(t) &= t \\ u''(t) - u'(t) + 3u(t) &= t \end{align*}
We look for a particular solution of the form $u_p(t) = At+B$. Then
\begin{align*} u_p''(t) - u_p'(t) + 3u_p(t) &= t \\ 0 - A + 3(At+B) &= t \\ (3A) t + (3B-A) &= t \end{align*}
Comparing coefficients, we have $3A = 1$ and $3B-A = 0$. Thus $A = \frac{1}{3}$ and $3B - \frac{1}{3} = 0$, so $3B = \frac{1}{3}$ and $B = \frac{1}{9}$. Thus $u_p(t) = \frac{1}{3} t + \frac{1}{9}$.
Then $u(t) = u_p(t) = \frac{1}{3} t + \frac{1}{9}$.
Therefore, $x(t) = (\frac{1}{3} t + \frac{1}{9}) e^{-t}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{(\frac{1}{3} t + \frac{1}{9}) e^{-t}}$

